-- title:
--   Theorem 5.16 — Slater soundness on the full Fock space: $q(\psi)\ge\frac16\mathrm{dist}(\psi,\mathcal S)^2$
-- statement:
--   For every state $|\psi\rangle$ of the $n$-mode fermionic Fock space $\bigwedge\mathbb C^n$, the two-copy Slater tester $\Pi_{\mathrm{test},\mathcal S}=\Pi_{\ker K}$ has rejection probability
--
--   $$
--   q(\psi)\ \ge\ \frac16\,\mathrm{dist}(\psi,\mathcal S)^2\qquad(5.15)
--   $$
--
--   for every $n$. No particle-number promise is made: $\psi$ may be a superposition across particle-number sectors and parities.
--
--   Theorem 3.5 cannot be applied on the whole Fock space because $\Pi_{\mathrm{test},\mathcal S}$ is permutation-invariant only on the equal-number sectors; this theorem supplies the soundness bound that Theorem 5.1 needs.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - encoding: the fermionic Fock space ⋀ℂⁿ and the bosonic Fock space ⋁ℂⁿ → ℓ²(Fin n → Fin 2) (Jordan–Wigner, 1 = occupied, 0-based modes) and ℓ²(Fin n → ℕ); ℋ^{⊗k} is ℓ²(Fin k → ι); reason: unitarily the same spaces; Lean convention
--   - encoding: classes of pure states (rays) → sets of unit vectors closed under global phase (Coh_n gets an explicit e^{iθ}, G_f an explicit phase in its orbit); reason: dist(ψ, 𝒞) is phase-invariant, so no distance changes
--   - encoding: fermionic Gaussian unitaries, generated by Hamiltonians quadratic in c, c† (p.17) → finite products exp(A₁)⋯exp(A_L) with A_l = ¼ Σ_{p,q} h⁽ˡ⁾_{pq} γ_p γ_q, h⁽ˡ⁾ real antisymmetric (Gf, Slater ⊆ Gf); reason: a Hermitian quadratic Hamiltonian is (i/4) Σ h_{pq} γ_p γ_q + c·I with h real antisymmetric, so the group it generates is exactly these products up to the phase e^{−ic}
--   - encoding: the two-copy space ⋀ℂⁿ ⊗ ⋀ℂⁿ and the two-copy operators A ⊗ B of (4.1) and Definition 5.2 → Kronecker products of the Jordan–Wigner matrices on Fin 2 → (Fin n → Fin 2) (kron2, copy 0 = first factor); reason: the paper's ⊗ is the ungraded tensor product (Lemma 5.15 decomposes ⋀ℂⁿ ⊗ ⋀ℂⁿ = ⊕ H_a ⊗ H_b; the proof of Proposition 5.5 factors ⟨Ψ|G†G|Ψ⟩ copy by copy)
-- source:
--   Iyer, O'Gorman, Parekh, Thompson, Zhao, Optimal testing of fermionic and bosonic Gaussian states, arXiv:2609.39113 (sha256 6683e57b…), Theorem 5.16, eq. (5.15), p.52

import Definitions.Def_GaussTesting_Slater
import Definitions.Def_GaussTesting_testSlater
import Definitions.Def_GaussTesting_distTo
import Definitions.Def_GaussTesting_rejProb

namespace GaussTesting

/-- **Theorem 5.16 (Soundness for arbitrary states).** For every `|ψ⟩ ∈ ⋀ℂⁿ`, the two-copy Slater tester
has rejection probability at least `q(ψ) ≥ (1/6) dist(ψ, 𝒮)²` (5.15).

Source: Iyer, O'Gorman, Parekh, Thompson, Zhao, *Optimal testing of fermionic and bosonic Gaussian states*, arXiv:2609.39113 (sha256 6683e57b…), Theorem 5.16, eq. (5.15), p.52.

**Formalization Note.** `q(ψ) = 1 − ⟨ψ^{⊗2}|Π_{ker K}|ψ^{⊗2}⟩` (`rejProb (testSlater n)`), for every unit
vector of the full Fock space (no particle-number promise), uniformly in `n`. -/
theorem theorem_5_16 (n : ℕ) (ψ : Ell2 (FermiCap.Gaussian.Qubits n)) (hψ : ‖ψ‖ = 1) :
    (1 / 6 : ℝ) * distTo ψ (Slater n) ^ 2 ≤ rejProb (testSlater n) ψ := by sorry

end GaussTesting
